TIME & SPACE COMPLEXITY ANALYSIS

Let:
N = number of words in dictionary
L = average length of dictionary words
T = length of target word

YOUR SOLUTION (STREAM + SORT APPROACH)

High-level steps:

Compute Levenshtein distance for each dictionary word

Store (word, editDistance) pairs in a list

Sort the list using:

Primary key: edit distance

Secondary key: word length

Return the first element

Time Complexity:

Edit distance per word = O(L × T)

For N words = O(N × L × T)

Sorting N elements = O(N log N)

Total Time Complexity:
O(N × L × T + N log N)

Space Complexity:

DP table per word = O(L × T)

Dictionary list storage = O(N)

Total Space Complexity:
O(L × T + N)

ChatGPT SOLUTION (SINGLE PASS MIN TRACKING)

High-level steps:

For each word:

Compute edit distance

Compare with current minimum

Update answer immediately (no sorting)

Return best word

Time Complexity:

Edit distance per word = O(L × T)

For N words = O(N × L × T)

No sorting

Total Time Complexity:
O(N × L × T)

Space Complexity:

DP table = O(L × T)

No extra list

Total Space Complexity:
O(L × T)

WHICH SOLUTION IS BETTER?

Winner: SINGLE PASS MIN TRACKING APPROACH(ChatGpt)

Reasons:

Avoids sorting (O(N log N) overhead)

Uses less auxiliary memory

it does not store the elements or Levenshtein val of all, instead it calculates dynamically

Faster for large dictionaries

Preferred in interviews due to clarity and efficiency

Your solution is:

Absolutely correct

Clean and readable

Slightly less optimal due to sorting

Interviewers will still accept it, but may ask:
"Can you do this without sorting?"

WHEN IS YOUR APPROACH ACCEPTABLE?

Your approach is fine when:

N is small

Readability is prioritized

You want clean functional-style code

Interview is not strict on micro-optimizations



Is there an EVEN MORE OPTIMIZED approach?
✅ Yes — Space Optimized DP (Advanced but impressive)
1)
You can optimize space from O(L × T) → O(min(L, T))

Idea:

Edit distance DP only depends on:

previous row

current row

So we can use two 1D arrays instead of a full matrix

Optimized Space Complexity
O(min(L, T))

Time Complexity (same)
O(N × L × T)


This is gold-level interview optimization, but not always required unless asked.


